"explanation": "Universal HIV screening is recommended for ALL pregnant women at the first prenatal visit per ACOG and CDC guidelines, regardless of risk factors. HIV (D) is correct because early identification allows antiretroviral therapy to reduce vertical transmission to less than 1%. While the patient has a cat (toxoplasmosis risk), toxoplasmosis is not a routine prenatal screen; her history of multiple partners makes STI screening relevant, but the single most important universal screen here is HIV.",
