"discipline": "Pediatrics",
"explanation": "A 3-week-old with direct hyperbilirubinemia, acholic stools, hepatosplenomegaly, and markedly elevated transaminases has biliary atresia until proven otherwise. Option B (abdominal ultrasound) is correct as the initial imaging step to evaluate for biliary atresia by assessing the gallbladder and biliary tree and to rule out choledochal cyst. If ultrasound is inconclusive, the workup proceeds to hepatobiliary scintigraphy (HIDA scan) and liver biopsy; surgical Kasai portoenterostomy must occur before 8 weeks for best outcome.",
