"explanation": "A 43-year-old woman with recurrent chest pain, transient ST depressions, negative troponins, and normal coronary arteries on catheterization has vasospastic (Prinzmetal) angina. Option A (diltiazem) is correct because calcium channel blockers are the mainstay of treatment for vasospastic angina by preventing coronary artery smooth muscle spasm. Beta-blockers like propranolol (C) are relatively contraindicated in Prinzmetal angina because they allow unopposed alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction, potentially worsening spasm.",
